THE LAST FAILING TEST, AND IT WAS RIGHT TO FAIL

With TEST-01 in, lib/floor-assembly.test.ts finally ran: 64 tests, 63 green.
The one red was `does not send a width, because nothing reads one`, and the
temptation is to call it a stale assertion and relax it. It is not stale. It
is a live guard whose premise was inverted underneath it, and following the
guard's own reasoning gives the opposite of what it currently asserts.

Its comment, and the matching one in assemblyDesignHref:

    WIDTH IS DELIBERATELY NOT SENT. FloorConfigurator reads exactly four
    parameters — species, finish, pattern, sqft — and has no board-width
    control at all, so `width=` would be a parameter nobody reads.

True when written. DESIGN-01 made it false: the configurator grew a
board-width control as step 04, reads `q.get('width')`, and validates it
against BOARD_WIDTHS exactly the way it validates finish and pattern.

So the parameter stopped being unread and started being DROPPED, and the
silent drop this file documents twice began happening in the one direction
nothing was watching. The homepage has had a board-width picker since VIS-07.
A visitor chose 3¼″ strip, watched the boards narrow in the picture, pressed
"Specify this floor", and arrived at /design on 5″ plank with no trace of the
choice they had just made and just seen.

The principle never moved — do not hand over what nothing reads, and do not
drop what somebody chose. Only which way it pointed moved.

THE FIX IS THREE ENDS, BECAUSE TWO WOULD HAVE LEFT THE SAME HOLE

  · assemblyDesignHref takes a widthId and sends it, validated against
    BOARD_WIDTHS — same shape as finish and pattern, so a stale or hand-edited
    link asking for a board we do not cut is dropped at the source rather than
    ignored on arrival.
  · FloorAssembly passes the width the visitor actually selected. It was
    already computing it for the picture and simply not handing it over.
  · The test asserts BOTH ENDS. This is the part that matters: the old
    assertion could only see one file, which is exactly how it went stale
    without anybody noticing. The replacement checks that the link carries the
    width AND that the page on the other side reads it AND validates it
    against the same list. Either half going missing re-opens the drop, and
    neither half is visible from the other file.

Two assertions were added beside it rather than folded in, so a regression
says which half broke: a width the catalogue does not contain is still
dropped, and a call with no width still sends none.

NO GUARD WAS WEAKENED. One assertion was inverted because the fact it asserted
is gone, and it came back covering strictly more than before — one file became
two, one claim became three.

AN INCOMPATIBLE COMBINATION IS SAFE TO SEND

Herringbone at 8″ is a real thing a person can select on the homepage and not
a floor we lay. It is sent anyway, because FloorConfigurator already repairs
whatever arrives through withAxis and tells the visitor what it moved and why.
Silently refusing to carry the choice would be the drop again, one level down.

VERIFIED

  All 96 species x pattern x width combinations the homepage can produce were
  generated and checked: every one yields a link /design honours, and all 24
  that arrive incompatible self-repair through withAxis to a layable floor.
  All four board widths round-trip by id (3-25, 5, 7, 8-plus); id 11 is
  dropped.
  Every pre-existing assertion in that describe block re-checked by hand:
  species, finish, pattern, the rejected finish, the rejected pattern.
  65 of 66 guards pass; verify:css fails only in this sandbox for want of
  node_modules and passed in the Codespace on the last three runs.

